  i think lavon probably would agree with  this too in fact but um  i mean the problem with trust in general  is it there there are a lot of draws and  there are a lot of positions now that  are  i mean when you look at them with  computers they're just very equal um  and while i still do enjoy classical  chess a lot i mean i've  always been a fan of fischer random and  okay i actually prefer it in general  okay thank you next question  question to magnus again yeah  annan said that preparing for a world  championship is different  than preparing for a tournament  do you agree with this and if so what  changing  in your training and in your routine if  if any and if this tournament was part  of your training  how you benefited from it  um i am i do believe that  um training for the world championship  is a little bit  uh different from other tournaments but  it it's also  true that if if something's working for  you you shouldn't  change it too much um  and uh as as for this tournament  i think it's been uh it's been a good  uh very good experience for me also as  us as training for  for the world championship uh because um  for me not playing a tournament since  the last time i played in  in tall memorial in in june till now  would be would be just a bit long and i  would  risk being being rusted there but here  uh  um i i was at a lot of challenges  uh for instance today i think was  was um was much of the same  kind of pressure situation that  will i think occur in a world  championship match  thank you very much uh dirk  um one for magnus um it's not a big  secret that you don't like to get up too  early  here the um the rounds they started at  one but on the other hand there was a  seven hour difference with  norway did you feel that you were  playing early in the evening  and if so did that help you  i didn't think too much about that i  felt that i had a  pretty healthy sleeping rhythm here  and i didn't need to get up  any earlier today really than i  i did the other days nevertheless i was  feeling a little bit sluggish today at  the start i don't know why but  i didn't think about it maybe it's uh  maybe it's  a bit of a factor i don't know okay  we'll have two final questions here  and then we will go on from there  uh when you reach this level of chess  play  do you guys play for fun anymore and if  so  do you play a computer or do you have  someone in particular that you enjoy  playing chess with  not a trick question  okay i guess i'll answer this one um in  general i don't think we play for fun  but all of us every so often we do  exhibitions or  simultaneous um well simultaneous  exhibitions and  uh that then it is somewhat fun i mean i  know for me personally i i don't  play for fun as kind of training per se  but you know when i do have the chance  um  i do do like just playing for fun every  so often here at the club with uh  regulars or also when i'm in europe  sometimes with with little kids so  it's it's just doing something fun i  mean trust of course it's been great to  all of us so it's good to give back  sometimes  okay and our final question  occasionally we read about people who  may be  cheating or are cheating at chess not  quite at your level  but what i'm what i'm wondering is are  you worried that somebody  very high rated 2700 feet a plus will  eventually start to cheat and it won't  be noticeable because  they'll be 27.50 then they're 28 20 like  oh they got better  and have you do you think fide should do  anything  about what seems to be a plethora of  incidents where people may be cheating  to  with with computers and glasses and ear  things and all that kind of stuff and  have you encountered any of that kind of  stuff  i mean that's such a difficult question  we couldn't even answer easy ones so  yeah but generally i mean now i'll take  this one i  i think  nobody who becomes a good player  you know has that kind of character to  do that  you know it's just it's just respectful  towards the game  and most of us we love the game of chess  and  you can't become strong and and then be  a cheater  you'll be a cheater in the start and  then  it's easier to to see that you are but  in general i think the problem is  we're talking about that too much  because i think there's  maybe zero one one percent of people  who would ever have uh  enough moral  ground to do that you know most of us  are  we love the game and we're distant human  beings  i'd like to thank the players for uh for  being so candid in their replies  um i know that these will show up in all  of the chess periodicals and websites uh  probably still this evening  um and  one of the reasons why we host these big  events at the chess club is basically  that  spotlighting the very best whether  they're the very best american players  or the best overall or  or you know from anywhere creates  or or showcases the role models uh for  small children all too often uh you have  little kids that look up to their  baseball or their football or their  basketball heroes um and while that  is also an important part of one's  character  it's also nice to see individuals who  are  famous who are celebrities of sorts  for being intellectual for using their  brains so  by hosting these events our goal is to  ultimately  introduce the game to as many students  as possible and let them know that  that they have other options you know  they don't just have to become a rap  star  or a basketball player or a baseball  player you know they can  become a chess player and then join us  here at the chess club  so i think we need just a couple of  minutes uh from our  camera folks uh and then we will  continue with the rest of our awards  ceremony  so if you can just be with us for maybe  two or three minutes we'll be ready to  go soon  you have a good chest joke  barnes and noble here in st louis and i  would go on wednesdays after school and  set up our little folding tables and i  would set out our coffee can for  